  "response": "## The finding: `crime_00007` — Missing Agent Context\n\n`crimes scan --format json` says **no**: the repo ships zero agent-readable instruction files.\n\n```\ncrime_00007  missing_agent_context  \"Missing Agent Context\"\n  severity: medium (0.6)   confidence: 0.90   agent_risk: 0.47\n  pack: universal          effort: small\n```\n\nEvidence it gives:\n\n- no `AGENTS.md` at repo root\n- no `CLAUDE.md` at repo root\n- no `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- no `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md`\n- `package.json` declares bins `tsup`, `tsup-node` — **agents have no way to discover commands**\n\nI confirmed this against the tree: root has `README.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `docs/`, but nothing in the four locations the detector checks.\n\nTwo notes on reading this finding:\n\n- It's a **repo-level** finding anchored to an arbitrary file (`assets/cjs_shims.js`). Don't go looking for a problem in that file — the subject is the repo root.\n- At `agent_risk` 0.47 it ranks **7th of 92** despite being only medium severity, and it's the single highest-ranked non-code finding. Everything above it is one file's problem; this one taxes every edit.\n\n## Why this matters for your first PR\n\nThe consequence isn't \"the docs are thin.\" It's that the scan itself shows a repo whose real conventions are *only* discoverable by reading code — and an agent that can't discover them will make confident, wrong edits:\n\n- **Build is not optional before test.** `pnpm test` = `pnpm run build && vitest run`. Tests execute the built CLI out of `dist/`, so an agent that edits `src/` and runs `vitest run` directly tests stale output and reports a false pass. Nothing in the repo tells it this.\n- **Commands are invisible.** `build-fast`, `test-only`, `dev`, `release` (bumpp) exist only in `package.json` scripts. An agent will guess `npm test` or `tsc` and get it wrong. pnpm 10.22 is pinned via `packageManager`; npm/yarn will produce a broken tree.\n- **The release process is non-obvious and destructive if guessed.** `CONTRIBUTING.md` says PRs merge to `dev`, then `dev` → `main`. An agent defaulting to \"open PR against main\" fights the workflow.\n- **The rest of the scan compounds it.** `src/index.ts` `build()` is 293 lines, `runEsbuild()` 237, `src/utils.ts` has fan-in 18, and 12 tests contain **zero assertions** (`crime_00061`–`crime_00072`). Large functions plus a test suite that partly proves nothing means an agent gets weak feedback on a wide blast radius — exactly the situation where written conventions are load-bearing.\n- **Optional peer deps are a trap.** `@microsoft/api-extractor`, `@swc/core`, `postcss`, `typescript` are all optional peers. An agent that adds a hard import to one breaks installs for users who didn't opt in, and nothing in the repo flags that boundary.\n\n## Minimal `AGENTS.md` that clears the finding\n\nThe detector is satisfied by an `AGENTS.md` at the repo root (or `CLAUDE.md`, or a `SKILL.md` under `.claude/skills/` / `.agents/skills/`). `AGENTS.md` is the right pick — it's tool-neutral, so it serves Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, and the rest from one file. Effort is rated **small**; this is one file, no code change.\n\nGrounded in what's actually in this repo:\n\n````markdown\n# AGENTS.md\n\ntsup — bundle a TypeScript library with no config, powered by esbuild.\nPublished to npm as `tsup`, exposing the `tsup` and `tsup-node` binaries.\n\n## Commands\n\nPackage manager is **pnpm** (pinned: `pnpm@10.22.0`). Do not use npm or yarn.\nNode >= 18.\n\n| Task            | Command              | Notes                                    |\n| --------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| Install         | `pnpm install`       |                                          |\n| Build           | `pnpm build`         | tsup builds itself into `dist/`          |\n| Build (no dts)  | `pnpm build-fast`    | much faster; use while iterating         |\n| Watch           | `pnpm dev`           | `build-fast --watch`                     |\n| Test            | `pnpm test`          | **builds first**, then runs vitest       |\n| Test (no build) | `pnpm test-only`     | only valid if `dist/` is already current |\n| Format          | `pnpm format`        | Prettier; run before committing          |\n\n**The build step is not optional.** Tests in `test/` invoke the built CLI from\n`dist/`, not `src/`. Running `pnpm test-only` after editing `src/` tests stale\noutput and will report a false pass. Use `pnpm test` unless you know `dist/`\nis current.\n\n## Layout\n\n- `src/index.ts` — public API and the `build()` orchestrator\n- `src/cli-main.ts` — CLI entry; `cli-default.ts` / `cli-node.ts` are the two bins\n- `src/esbuild/` — esbuild pipeline and plugins (postcss, svelte, swc, …)\n- `src/rollup.ts`, `src/rollup/` — dts bundling via rollup\n- `src/tsc.ts`, `src/api-extractor.ts` — declaration-file paths\n- `src/plugins/` — tsup's own plugin system (cjs-interop, terser, tree-shaking, …)\n- `src/utils.ts`, `src/log.ts` — shared helpers; widely imported, edit conservatively\n- `test/` — integration tests that shell out to the built CLI\n- `schema.json` — JSON schema for `tsup.config.ts`; update it when adding an option\n\n## Conventions\n\n- Add a test in `test/` for every behaviour change. Assert on real output —\n  several existing tests only run setup without asserting; don't copy that shape.\n- `@microsoft/api-extractor`, `@swc/core`, `postcss`, and `typescript` are\n  **optional peer dependencies**. Import them lazily and guard for absence;\n  a top-level import breaks installs for users who didn't opt in.\n- New config options need three updates: the type in `src/options.ts`, the CLI\n  flag in `src/cli-main.ts`, and `schema.json`.\n- Formatting is Prettier-owned. Don't hand-format; run `pnpm format`.\n\n## Git workflow\n\nPRs target the **`dev`** branch, not `main`. Releases go `dev` → `main`, then a\nGitHub Action publishes to npm. Don't run `pnpm release` (bumpp) yourself.\n\n## Don't\n\n- Don't edit `dist/` — it's generated output.\n- Don't add ESLint; Prettier is the only style tool here.\n- Don't bump the version manually; `bumpp` owns it in the release flow.\n````\n\nDrop that at the repo root and `crime_00007` disappears on the next scan. It won't touch the other 91 findings — but it's the one that changes how every future agent edit starts.",
